; Tutorial_0_Overview.inix -- what DbDo is, the databases it comes with, two reader keys, and the table of contents.

[feed]
Title=DbDo Walkthroughs
Description=Spoken walks through DbDo, the database program built for working by ear, and the ready-made databases that come with it. Ten walks, each under five minutes, in the order a new person meets them.
Author=Jamal Mazrui
Email=jamal.mazrui@outlook.com
Base=

[about]
Title=0 - Overview
Intro=What DbDo is, the ready-made databases that come with it, three keys of the screen reader's own, and the table of contents.
Setup=Nothing is needed; this walk is listened to.
Homework=Take walks one and two, then the walk for the database you want first.

[step]

Say=DbDo is a database program for people who listen to the screen.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=It shows one table at a time as a list of records you move through by row and by column. Every cell can be heard, edited, searched, marked and counted from the keyboard, and the program answers questions about where you are without changing anything.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=It also comes with ready-made databases, called templates, each a complete example you can use as it is or make your own. These walks are built around them.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Radio comes first: RadioTrail, internet radio stations from around the world, each one a key press from playing.
Key=
Hear=49186 stations from 219 countries.

[step]

Say=Then JobTrail, for a job search: openings, the people at each employer, what you sent and what happened.
Key=
Hear=Jobs, contacts, actions, documents and stories, linked to one another.

[step]

Say=BookTrail, a catalog of what you read and want to read, in any form.
Key=
Hear=Audio from BARD, text from Bookshare, braille and eBooks, side by side.

[step]

Say=And MusicTrail, the greatest albums of ten genres and the people who made them.
Key=
Hear=100 albums and 270 performers, each album linked to who recorded it.

[step]

Say=Four more get a shorter visit in walk seven: TechTraining, BlindCreations, ContactTrail and RecipeTrail.
Key=
Hear=

[step]
Say=Two voices take these walks. I am the host; the other voice plays the screen reader. It is a simulation, so your own reader's words may differ a little.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=You know your screen reader's own keys already, and three of them are worth remembering whenever you feel lost. Insert plus Up Arrow says the line you are on again.
Key=Insert+Up Arrow
Hear=ACB Media 1, The United States Of America, ACB Media, blindness, Row 1 of 49186
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Insert plus Tab says what has the focus: its name, its role, and its value or state.
Key=Insert+Tab
Hear=Records list view, ACB Media 1, 1 of 49186
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Insert plus T says the title of the window.
Key=Insert+T
Hear=DbDo, RadioTrail dot d b, stations
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=On a laptop keyboard, Caps Lock usually stands in for Insert, though in some laptop layouts the line command moves to a letter, such as Caps Lock plus L. And if speech goes by before you catch it, your reader can replay it: Insert plus X in some readers, a speech history in others.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Now the table of contents. I say the number and the title; the reader says what the walk covers.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=One, User Interface.
Key=
Hear=The window, the record list, the Say keys that ask without changing, the menus, the rules every key follows, and where help lives.

[step]

Say=Two, Install and Tune In.
Key=
Hear=The installer, DbDo opening on RadioTrail, a station found by name and playing.

[step]

Say=Three, Find a Station.
Key=
Hear=A station that may carry the Seahawks game: searched, filtered, narrowed, put in order and played.

[step]

Say=Four, Track Job Leads.
Key=
Hear=JobTrail: a status brought up to date, a job added, notes kept, and the people linked to a job.

[step]

Say=Five, Catalog Your Books.
Key=
Hear=BookTrail: what you are reading next, counts by format, and a reading log written as a document.

[step]

Say=Six, Explore Music.
Key=
Hear=MusicTrail: from an album to the band that recorded it, to its members, and back.

[step]

Say=Seven, More Templates.
Key=
Hear=TechTraining, BlindCreations, ContactTrail and RecipeTrail, and bringing your copy up to date when a template improves.

[step]

Say=Eight, Ask with SQL.
Key=
Hear=A question in the database's own language, and the same question asked in plain words.

[step]

Say=Nine, Conclusion.
Key=
Hear=What to carry away, the words DbDo uses, and the help built in.
